1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,750
Inside almost every one of your
cells is about two

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:04,720
metres of DNA.

3
00:00:05,375 --> 00:00:08,645
Stretched out, the DNA from a
single cell would be taller

4
00:00:08,655 --> 00:00:09,455
than you.

5
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,930
Yet it fits inside a nucleus
just a few thousandths

6
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:14,100
of a millimetre across.

7
00:00:14,583 --> 00:00:17,653
It's wound around tiny protein
spools, then

8
00:00:17,663 --> 00:00:18,133
coiled,

9
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:21,603
and coiled again, like a phone
cord twisted on itself.

10
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:24,365
And it comes split into
forty-six pieces,

11
00:00:24,725 --> 00:00:25,525
called chromosomes.

12
00:00:26,208 --> 00:00:29,418
Line up the DNA from all your
cells, and it would

13
00:00:29,428 --> 00:00:32,568
stretch billions of kilometres,
farther than Neptune.

14
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:35,205
And every time one of your cells
divides,

15
00:00:35,905 --> 00:00:39,325
all of it unpacks, copies
itself, and packs back up.

16
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:43,095
Two metres of instructions,
folded into a speck too small

17
00:00:43,105 --> 00:00:43,905
to see.
